 format(2000); // "2s"
 ```
 
+### Compound Durations
+
+`parseCompound` sums several `<number><unit>` pieces. Pieces can be separated by spaces, commas, or nothing at all.
+
+```ts
+import { parseCompound } from 'ms';
+
+parseCompound('1h 30m');        // 5400000
+parseCompound('1h, 30m');       // 5400000
+parseCompound('2 days 4 hours') // 187200000
+parseCompound('1h30m15s');      // 5415000
+```
+
+Unlike `parse`, it throws a `TypeError` (with the offending input in the message) instead of returning `NaN` when the input can't be parsed. A bare number is only allowed on its own (`parseCompound('100')`), not alongside other pieces (`'1h 30'` throws).
+
 ### Enhanced Type Safety
 
 If you want strict type checking for the input value, you can use `parseStrict`.
